izens in that book between  relationships between the rich and the  poor and how a kind of economic  dimension  cross-cut in a way or blunted the the  class conflict that might otherwise  ensue in very unequal societies and so  the argument was that oil resources oil  rents and the jargon provide a source of  revenue that that funds public spending  without forcing the state to to impose  redistributive taxes so it was about oil  but it was really about social relations  and particularly relations between  classes so I think this this idea about  how different cleavage dimensions or  dimensions of interest or identity  relate to one another and shaped  political outcomes this basically what  what I what I've been studying for a  long time and this is in a in a sort of  different manifestation I taught a class  here at Yale and on ethnic politics in  comparative perspective and had a very  talented undergraduate in my class who  was interested in the phenomenon of  Christina Hutchins wrote a an excellent  term paper about it so I suggested that  we we study cruising --oj systematically  and try to assess whether there really  is an influence of kusanagi on on  political behavior and then in turn on  sort of broader political outcomes so  Lauren Harrison who is Yale College  class of 2009 was my co-author on the  paper that I'm talking about today okay  and for the paper you developed an  experimental research design unlike any  others how is it different well what we  did in in bamako which is Molly's  capital is was to canvass all of the  neighborhoods in Bamako and and take a  sample of participants for a study and  then we showed participants videotaped  political speeches they were intended to  be as much like a routine political  speech that that a Malian would  encounter in any campaign any kind of  legislative campaign and because last  name or surname in Mali indicates both  ethnicity and linkages and the kucinich  networks by manipulating or changing  this t