nge was idiosyncratic to Mali but  the idea of using political speeches and  manipulating the relationship between  subjects and the politician along not  just one but but sort of multiple  dimensions I think is exportable and so  I've been doing a series of studies  actually using a very similar design in  in India most recently in India South  Africa and Brazil as well so those in  addition the Mali project are projects  that I've been working on well on  recently on sabbatical from research  sabbatical from teaching and and they're  the design is very similar so I think it  does provide kind of  useful tool for thinking about how  different dimensions of interest or  identity might shape political  preferences very good well thank you so  much for being here with us today and  sharing some of your research thank you  so much for having me for more  information about Professor Dunning and  his work please visit our website at  yale.edu McMillan Report be sure to join  us again for another episode of the  McMillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  McMillan Center for International and  area studies at yam  you

